# Allowlist for check-public-artifact.sh.
#
# This repository DOCUMENTS the workstream convention, so its published examples
# are the exact strings the sweep must flag in every other repo. Entries are
# scoped to a file AND to the specific example identifier, never to a whole file:
# a genuine leak appearing in one of these same files -- a private project name,
# an absolute workspace path, a real task ID -- still reports.
#
# The sweep prints the number of lines it suppressed on every run, so growth here
# is visible rather than inferred. Keep it that way: widen an entry only when a
# hit is genuinely published content, and never to quieten a real finding.

# The state-file format rule, which shows a task pointer, a backlog and a gate.
^\.claude/rules/workstreams-rule\.md:[0-9]+:.*#(DR-[0-9]|G-DR)\b
^\.claude/rules/workstreams-rule\.md:[0-9]+:.*#MG-2\b

# The workstream template ships the Completion phase as literal tasks.
^\.claude/skills/workstream-create/templates/workstream\.md:[0-9]+:.*#CL-[123]\b

# The acceptance test drives a demo workstream end to end, so it names that
# workstream's own tasks and its state path. Both are fixtures, not references.
^tests/va3-acceptance\.sh:[0-9]+:.*#(BD-[0-9]|G-BD)\b
^tests/va3-acceptance\.sh:[0-9]+:.*\.state/workstreams/project/greeting-script\b

# The offline hook fixture builds three demo workstreams and generates their
# task lines, so it names fixture IDs and fixture state paths.
^tests/session-start-acceptance\.sh:[0-9]+:.*#(BD-[0-9]+|G-B)
^tests/session-start-acceptance\.sh:[0-9]+:.*\$T/\.state/workstreams/

# --- range-mode forms --------------------------------------------------------
# `--range` is the mode the pre-push sweep uses, and it labels every hit with
# the RANGE rather than the file, so none of the file-anchored entries above can
# match there. These repeat the same exemptions anchored on content instead.
#
# They are necessarily broader than the file-scoped forms, so each is pinned to
# the exact published string it exempts rather than to a bare identifier: a
# pattern like `#BD-[0-9]+` alone would suppress a genuine reference anywhere in
# the tree. The durable fix belongs in the sweep script -- emitting file:line in
# range mode would let one file-anchored entry serve both modes -- and that
# script is not this repository's to change.

# The workstream template ships the Completion phase as literal task lines.
:[0-9]+:[-+ ]?- \[[ x]\] #CL-[123]: (Disposition all Learnings|Verify deletion criteria|Archive \(`\.state/workstreams/ARCHIVE\.md` line)

# The rule's ACTIVE.md example shows a task pointer; its line moved when the
# example lost its trailing comments.
:[0-9]+:[-+ ]?task: "#DR-2 - state-file formats"

# The Task IDs bullet IS the convention's definition; its examples are the form
# being defined, not references to anything.
:[0-9]+:[-+ ]?- \*\*Task IDs\*\*: `#XX-N`

# The end-to-end test's closure prompt names the demo workstream's own gate,
# completion tasks and archive tag as the scenario it drives. Pinned to the
# prompt's exact opening words, and to the one hunk header that quotes the
# gate-check label, so an edit to that line does not report its own fixtures.
# Same hole as every line-level entry: a private name written INTO that prompt
# would be hidden; the prompt is read whenever it is edited.
:[0-9]+:[-+ ]?claude -p --dangerously-skip-permissions 'As the user I approve the #G-BD gate: the deliverable works\.
:[0-9]+:@@ .*@@ check "stopped AT gate \(G-BD unchecked\)"

# Acceptance-test fixtures: generated task lines and temp-directory state paths.
:[0-9]+:[-+ ]?.*echo "- \[[ x]\] #(BD-[0-9]+|G-B)
# The hook test's GATE-READY checks grep for the two fixture gates they assert on,
# pinned to the check's own quoting so a real gate ID elsewhere still reports.
:[0-9]+:[-+ ]?  "grep -qE? '[^']*#G-B[XB]\.\*(SATISFIED|satisfied)'
:[0-9]+:[-+ ]?task: "#BD-1 - first task"
:[0-9]+:[-+ ]?.*"\$T/\.state/workstreams/
:[0-9]+:[-+ ]?.*\.state/workstreams/project/greeting-script\b

# The record script's test builds its fixture workstreams and ACTIVE.md as
# heredocs, so their task, gate and pointer lines appear bare; each entry pins
# the fixture's own words, so a real ID in a real sentence still reports.
^tests/record-acceptance\.sh:[0-9]+:.*#(DR-1|G-DR|BD-[12]|G-DN|G-BD)\b
:[0-9]+:[-+ ]?- \[ \] #DR-1: draft the design outline
:[0-9]+:[-+ ]?- \[ \] #G-DR: USER CHECKPOINT -- release readiness SATISFIED 2026-01-01 pending final confirmation
:[0-9]+:[-+ ]?  - \[ \] #BD-1: implement the core module
:[0-9]+:[-+ ]?- \[ \] #BD-2: blocked on feature/beta for the shared schema work
:[0-9]+:[-+ ]?- \[ \] #G-DN: USER CHECKPOINT -- pending review before shipment
:[0-9]+:[-+ ]?# contains "waiting for #G-BD"\.
:[0-9]+:[-+ ]?task: "#BD-2 - shared schema work"
:[0-9]+:[-+ ]?Release is waiting for #G-BD in feature/beta before we can proceed with the next steps here\.
:[0-9]+:[-+ ]?check "cross ref on the hold line has preceding_id #BD-2"

# This file's own entries quote the identifiers they exempt, so a diff that adds
# or edits one reports every string it names.
#
# Exempted by SHAPE -- a line whose content begins with a regex anchor, `^` for
# the file-anchored form or `:[0-9]+:` for the range-anchored one. Comments and
# prose in this file are NOT exempt, so a private name written into a comment
# here still reports; that was verified by injecting two.
#
# The tighter alternative, one exemption per entry naming its literal text, was
# tried and does not terminate: each entry that exempts an entry is itself an
# entry quoting the same identifier, so the file grows a new hit every time it
# grows a line. This shape terminates.
#
# THE HOLE IT LEAVES, stated because a suppression nobody can see is the failure
# this whole file risks: a private task ID written INTO AN ENTRY in this file
# would be hidden, since the entry matches by shape regardless of content.
# Nowhere else -- the same ID in a comment here, or anywhere in the tree,
# reports. Entries are read when added, and the sweep prints its suppression
# count every run, which is the compensating visibility. The real fix is for the
# sweep to emit file:lineno in range mode; a single `^\.public-sweep-allow:`
# entry would then cover this file by path and this block would be deleted.
^\.public-sweep-allow:[0-9]+:
:[0-9]+:[-+ ]?\^[\\a-zA-Z]
:[0-9]+:[-+ ]?:\[0-9\]\+:
